1
00:00:00,740 --> 00:00:02,299
You have reached the final module.

2
00:00:02,420 --> 00:00:06,579
Over the last six modules, we have built a massive enterprise foundation.

3
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:09,479
Now it's time to prepare for the certification.

4
00:00:09,899 --> 00:00:13,880
The Google Cloud Professional DevOps Engineer exam is unique

5
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:17,540
because it does not just test if you know the tools,

6
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:23,129
it tests if you can apply SRE principles to organizational challenges.

7
00:00:23,559 --> 00:00:28,209
Let's understand the specific question formats you will encounter and the

8
00:00:28,209 --> 00:00:31,850
strategies you need to navigate them under the pressure.

9
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:34,939
Most of the questions are multiple choice,

10
00:00:35,169 --> 00:00:37,810
but they are really simply definitions.

11
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:42,669
For example, instead of asking what is shared VPC in the exam,

12
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:44,330
you will be asked,

13
00:00:44,610 --> 00:00:48,930
your security team requires central control over networking while

14
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:53,009
giving developers autonomy over their compute resources.

15
00:00:53,130 --> 00:00:56,279
Which networking model should you use? Now in this

16
00:00:56,279 --> 00:00:59,959
architecture kind of a question, you have to look for the constraint.

17
00:01:00,209 --> 00:01:01,360
In this example,

18
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:06,510
the central control with autonomy is the hallmark of the shared VPC.

19
00:01:06,639 --> 00:01:09,110
Another one is a scenario‑based reasoning.

20
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:12,565
You will be presented with a company's current state,

21
00:01:12,864 --> 00:01:16,834
with generally a messy state and a desired future state.

22
00:01:17,035 --> 00:01:18,544
In these kinds of questions,

23
00:01:18,904 --> 00:01:23,904
many times you can solve this by using strategic elimination.

24
00:01:24,584 --> 00:01:28,805
You can often find the right answer by spotting the distractors.

25
00:01:29,105 --> 00:01:34,014
Another strategy that can be applied is the flag and move strategy,

26
00:01:34,305 --> 00:01:36,135
that is for your time management.

27
00:01:36,540 --> 00:01:40,160
You have 120 minutes for roughly 50‑60 questions.

28
00:01:40,379 --> 00:01:44,019
Do not let a three‑paragraph scenario derail your rhythm.

29
00:01:44,339 --> 00:01:48,720
If you don't know the answer in 60 seconds, flag it and move on.

30
00:01:49,069 --> 00:01:53,260
Once you've finished with all the questions, you can review it and work on it.
